[Applause]  it's been a year of extreme weather  events in all corners of the globe  but ahead of the cop26 climate summit  greta tunberg is zeroing in on the uk  so i'm really hoping that we will stop  referring to the uk as a climate leader  because if you look at the reality that  is simply not true the government says  it's made big strides towards reaching  net zero the uk has been able to cut our  own co2 emissions by about 42 percent on  1990 levels but the young activists  calls that claim into question  there is a lie  that the uk has is a climate leader and  that they have reduced their co2  emissions by 44 since 1990 or whatever  and of course if you don't include all  emissions that's  of course the  statistics are going to look much nicer  but if you include things like aviation  shipping outsourcing in the  um imports of  like consumption for instance and the  burning of biomass it doesn't really  look that good she was speaking at the  launch of a new unicef report it finds  almost half the world's children living  countries classed as being at extremely  high risk of climate change impacts  it says 2 billion children are highly  exposed to air pollution 920 million are  highly exposed to water scarcity  and 820 million to heat waves  the report coincides with the third  anniversary of greta's first fridays for  future school strike  a movement which started with a single  child has since been joined by millions  more  activists like greta say children are  the biggest victims of climate change  and it's the adults at cop26 who need to  lead the fight against it  jemima walker sky news

